de=3D"0">Modifies Data</b>: The client works locally.</p></li><li><p data-p=
ath-to-node=3D"26,2,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"26,2,0" data-index-in-node=
=3D"0">POST /resource/{id}/save</b>: Client sends data back. The server ver=
ifies the <code data-path-to-node=3D"26,2,0" data-index-in-node=3D"74">vers=
ion</code> and the <code data-path-to-node=3D"26,2,0" data-index-in-node=3D=
"90">Long Lock</code>.</p></li></ol><h3 data-path-to-node=3D"27">Why this w=
orks with Java 25 &amp; Spring Boot 4:</h3><ul data-path-to-node=3D"28"><li=
><p data-path-to-node=3D"28,0,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"28,0,0" data-index=
-in-node=3D"0">Virtual Threads:</b> Since <code data-path-to-node=3D"28,0,0=
" data-index-in-node=3D"23">LogicalLockService</code> involves database I/O=
, using Virtual Threads (standard in Java 25) ensures that waiting for a lo=
ck record doesn't pin OS threads, allowing your lock service to scale to th=
ousands of concurrent "waiters."</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"28,1,0=
"><b data-path-to-node=3D"28,1,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">No Database Bott=
lenecks:</b> By avoiding <code data-path-to-node=3D"28,1,0" data-index-in-n=
ode=3D"37">SELECT FOR UPDATE</code>, you don't hold database row locks open=
 during the user's "think time." The database remains responsive.</p></li><=
li><p data-path-to-node=3D"28,2,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"28,2,0" data-ind=
ex-in-node=3D"0">Safety:</b> If the user closes their browser, the <code da=
ta-path-to-node=3D"28,2,0" data-index-in-node=3D"46">expiryTime</code> in t=
he <code data-path-to-node=3D"28,2,0" data-index-in-node=3D"64">ResourceLoc=
k</code> table ensures the record isn't locked forever.</p></li></ul><p dat=
a-path-to-node=3D"29"><b data-path-to-node=3D"29" data-index-in-node=3D"0">=
Pro-Tip:</b> For high-scale environments, implement the <code data-path-to-=
node=3D"29" data-index-in-node=3D"52">LogicalLockService</code> using <b da=
ta-path-to-node=3D"29" data-index-in-node=3D"77">Redis</b> with TTL (Time-T=
o-Live) instead of a relational database table. This keeps the "Long Lock" =
traffic off your main ACID store.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"30">Would you =
