th-to-node=3D"11">3. Frontend Integration Flow</h2><p data-path-to-node=3D"=
12">To make this "invisible" to the user until something goes wrong, follow=
 this flow:</p><h3 data-path-to-node=3D"13">Phase A: The Checkout (Long Loc=
k)</h3><p data-path-to-node=3D"14">When the user clicks "Edit," the fronten=
d calls <code data-path-to-node=3D"14" data-index-in-node=3D"48">POST /api/=
resources/{id}/lock</code>.</p><ul data-path-to-node=3D"15"><li><p data-pat=
h-to-node=3D"15,0,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"15,0,0" data-index-in-node=3D"=
0">Response:</b> <code data-path-to-node=3D"15,0,0" data-index-in-node=3D"1=
0">{ "fencingToken": 105, "expiresAt": "2026-04-03T21:45:00Z" }</code></p><=
/li><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"15,1,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"15,1,0" dat=
a-index-in-node=3D"0">Action:</b> The frontend stores this <code data-path-=
to-node=3D"15,1,0" data-index-in-node=3D"33">fencingToken</code> in local s=
tate.</p></li></ul><h3 data-path-to-node=3D"16">Phase B: The Background Hea=
rtbeat</h3><p data-path-to-node=3D"17">Since we are using a RDBMS for locks=
, the frontend should occasionally "ping" a refresh endpoint to extend the =
<code data-path-to-node=3D"17" data-index-in-node=3D"111">expiryTime</code>=
 if the user is still typing.</p><ul data-path-to-node=3D"18"><li><p data-p=
ath-to-node=3D"18,0,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"18,0,0" data-index-in-node=
=3D"0">Tip:</b> Use Java 25's <b data-path-to-node=3D"18,0,0" data-index-in=
-node=3D"19">Virtual Threads</b> on the server side for these heartbeats. T=
hey are extremely "cheap" and won't bloat your thread pool even if thousand=
s of users are editing simultaneously.</p></li></ul><h3 data-path-to-node=
=3D"19">Phase C: The Save (Fencing)</h3><p data-path-to-node=3D"20">The fro=
ntend sends the payload + the <code data-path-to-node=3D"20" data-index-in-=
node=3D"37">X-Fencing-Token</code> header.</p><ul data-path-to-node=3D"21">=
<li><p data-path-to-node=3D"21,0,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"21,0,0" data-in=
dex-in-node=3D"0">Scenario 1 (Happy Path):</b> Token matches, DB version ma=
tches. Data is saved.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"21,1,0"><b data-p=
ath-to-node=3D"21,1,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">Scenario 2 (Lock Expired/St=
olen):</b> If the token in the DB is now <code data-path-to-node=3D"21,1,0"=
 data-index-in-node=3D"64">106</code> (because another admin forced a lock =
override), the server returns <b data-path-to-node=3D"21,1,0" data-index-in=
-node=3D"135">409 Conflict</b>.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"21,2,0"=
><b data-path-to-node=3D"21,2,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">Scenario 3 (Optim=
istic Version Mismatch):</b> If the fencing token is valid, but the <code d=
ata-path-to-node=3D"21,2,0" data-index-in-node=3D"81">@Version</code> field=
 in the DB changed (perhaps by a background system process), the server ret=
urns <b data-path-to-node=3D"21,2,0" data-index-in-node=3D"175">409 Conflic=
t</b>.</p></li></ul><hr data-path-to-node=3D"22"><h2 data-path-to-node=3D"2=
3">4. Handling the Conflict UI</h2><p data-path-to-node=3D"24">When the fro=
ntend receives the <b data-path-to-node=3D"24" data-index-in-node=3D"31">40=
9 Conflict</b> with the <code data-path-to-node=3D"24" data-index-in-node=
=3D"53">currentData</code>, you should provide a "Merge" view:</p><ol start=
=3D"1" data-path-to-node=3D"25"><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"25,0,0"><b data=
-path-to-node=3D"25,0,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">Show side-by-side:</b> "Y=
our Changes" vs. "Server Version."</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"25,1=
,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"25,1,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">Highlight diff=
erences:</b> Use a simple diffing library.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=
=3D"25,2,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"25,2,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">Resolu=
tion:</b> The user picks which values to keep. When they hit "Save" again, =
the frontend fetches a <b data-path-to-node=3D"25,2,0" data-index-in-node=
=3D"100">new</b> lock/token first, then submits the resolved data.</p></li>=
</ol><hr data-path-to-node=3D"26"><h2 data-path-to-node=3D"27">Summary of t=
he Design</h2><ul data-path-to-node=3D"28"><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"28,0=
,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"28,0,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">Short Lock:</b=
> Handled by JPA <code data-path-to-node=3D"28,0,0" data-index-in-node=3D"2=
7">@Version</code> (Standard Optimistic Locking).</p></li><li><p data-path-=
to-node=3D"28,1,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"28,1,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0"=
>Long Lock:</b> Handled by <code data-path-to-node=3D"28,1,0" data-index-in=
-node=3D"22">ResourceLock</code> table (prevents UI entry).</p></li><li><p =
data-path-to-node=3D"28,2,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"28,2,0" data-index-in-=
node=3D"0">Fencing Token:</b> Handled by an incrementing <code data-path-to=
-node=3D"28,2,0" data-index-in-node=3D"42">long</code> in the lock table (p=
revents "Zombie Client" overwrites).</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"28=
,3,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"28,3,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">Concurrency:=
</b> Handled by <b data-path-to-node=3D"28,3,0" data-index-in-node=3D"24">J=
ava 25 Virtual Threads</b> to keep the RDBMS lock-check and heartbeat logic=
 highly scalable.</p></li></ul><p data-path-to-node=3D"29">This approach is=
 significantly more robust than pessimistic locking because it doesn't leav=
e database transactions open while a human is "thinking" or "typing."</p></=
