TAMP</code> in Postgres/MySQL). This ensures a single "Source of Truth" for=
 time.</p><hr data-path-to-node=3D"14"><h2 data-path-to-node=3D"15">3. What=
 else can be done? (Advanced Improvements)</h2><h3 data-path-to-node=3D"16"=
>A. Idempotency Keys (The Network Glitch)</h3><p data-path-to-node=3D"17">W=
hat happens if the client sends a <code data-path-to-node=3D"17" data-index=
-in-node=3D"35">save()</code> request, the server processes it successfully=
, but the network drops before the client gets the <code data-path-to-node=
=3D"17" data-index-in-node=3D"138">200 OK</code>?
The client will likely retry. But since the previous <code data-path-to-nod=
e=3D"17" data-index-in-node=3D"199">save()</code> deleted the lock/token, t=
he retry will fail with a <b data-path-to-node=3D"17" data-index-in-node=3D=
"257">409 Conflict</b>.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"18"><b data-path-to-node=
=3D"18" data-index-in-node=3D"0">The Fix:</b> Store an <code data-path-to-n=
ode=3D"18" data-index-in-node=3D"18">idempotency_key</code> (usually a UUID=
 generated by the client) with the resource. If the server sees a request w=
ith a key it has already processed, it returns the cached success response =
instead of an error.</p><h3 data-path-to-node=3D"19">B. Audit Trails for "L=
ock Stealing"</h3><p data-path-to-node=3D"20">In enterprise apps, users get=
 frustrated when they lose a lock.</p><ul data-path-to-node=3D"21"><li><p d=
ata-path-to-node=3D"21,0,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"21,0,0" data-index-in-n=
ode=3D"0">Action:</b> When a fencing token causes a rejection, log <i data-=
